-- title:
--   Integrality of ̃ g/Δ̃^{ m} over ℂ[jmath̃]
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal G$ be a subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb R)$ which is arithmetic and all of whose elements have determinant one, and assume that the real number $1$ belongs to `𝒢.strictPeriods`, so that $q$-expansions of width $1$ are available for forms on $\mathcal G$. Let $m$ be a natural number, let $k$ be an integer with $k = 12m$, and let $g$ be a modular form of weight $k$ for $\mathcal G$. Write $\tilde h$ for the image in the field of Laurent series $\mathbb C((q))$ of the width-one $q$-expansion `qExpansion 1` of a form $h$, the expansions of the level-one forms $E_4$ and $\Delta$ being taken as such, with no restriction to $\mathcal G$ intervening. The assertion is that the Laurent series $\tilde g/\tilde\Delta^{\,m}$ is integral over the $\mathbb C$-subalgebra of $\mathbb C((q))$ generated by the single element $\tilde E_4^{\,3}/\tilde\Delta$, that is, over $\mathbb C[\tilde\jmath]$ up to the normalisation of $j$. No bound on the degree of a monic relation, and no relation to the index of $\mathcal G$, is asserted; the case $m = 0$ says that $\tilde g$ itself is integral over this subalgebra.
--
--   This is the monic (integrality) form of the classical statement that a modular function whose only poles are at the cusps is integral over $\mathbb C[j]$, here phrased entirely on the level of $q$-expansions in $\mathbb C((q))$ and for an arbitrary arithmetic group of determinant one admitting $1$ as a strict period. It is the source of integrality over $\mathbb C[\tilde\jmath]$ in the construction of models of modular curves, and is invoked by the results on integrality of coefficient embeddings of $\tilde\jmath$; its proof cites the level-one case [`ModularForm.exists_qExpansion_eq_aeval_mul_pow_levelOne`](thm.html#ModularForm.exists_qExpansion_eq_aeval_mul_pow_levelOne), which writes $\tilde F = P(\tilde E_4^{\,3}/\tilde\Delta)\,\tilde\Delta^{\,N}$ with $\deg P \le N$ for a level-one form $F$ of weight $12N$.
-- source:
